From: Crunch41 <70764418+Crunch41@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 04:01:24 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] Tune ENet delivery and timeouts for internet play

Local wireless play, which the room stands in for, is nearly lossless
and in order: the Wi-Fi MAC acknowledges and retransmits frames. Over
the internet the room has to choose how to relay game packets, and ENet's
defaults are tuned for neither case.

Relay delivery (EDEN_ROOM_RELAY_MODE):

- reliable: upstream's behaviour. Everything is retransmitted in order,
  so one lost packet holds up everything behind it (head-of-line
  blocking) on lossy, high-latency paths.
- sequenced: unreliable but in order; late packets are dropped. The
  closest match to local wireless delivery, and the first thing to try
  when a game stutters under reliable.
- unsequenced: unreliable and unordered; lowest latency, but games see
  reordering they never see on real hardware.

The image sets reliable. With EDEN_ROOM_RELAY_MODE unset, the legacy
EDEN_ROOM_RELAY_RELIABLE=1 selects reliable, and with neither set the
room uses unsequenced. Unknown values log a warning and use unsequenced.
The unreliable modes also set ENET_PACKET_FLAG_UNRELIABLE_FRAGMENT, as
without it ENet silently sends packets above its fragment size (about
1366 bytes, which large game packets exceed) as reliable fragments.
Only the room's sending side changes: Eden clients still send to the
room reliably, and control messages are always reliable.

Peer tuning, applied when a peer joins:

- ENet's packet throttle drops unreliable packets at random once a
  peer's RTT rises. A throttle deceleration of 0 keeps it at 100%, so
  the unreliable modes never lose game packets to it. (Reliable packets
  are not throttled, so this changes nothing in reliable mode.)
- Peer timeouts default to 12 s minimum and 60 s maximum instead of
  ENet's 5 s and 30 s, so a few seconds of loss on a long route do not
  drop players (EDEN_ROOM_PEER_TIMEOUT_MIN/MAX, in ms; the maximum is
  raised to the minimum if lower).
- Peers are pinged every 100 ms instead of 500 ms, which keeps RTT and
  loss statistics current (EDEN_ROOM_PING_INTERVAL, in ms).

Invalid timing values (0, non-numeric, above one hour) are ignored with a
warning. The channel count, the flush cadence and the relayed bytes are
unchanged.

Tests: tests/test_room_protocol.py runs the room in reliable mode, as the
image does, and checks relay, disconnect and reconnect.
